WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:04.556
This is the executive briefing
for Book 16 of the Federal

3
00:00:04.556 --> 00:00:08.350
Modernization Workbook,
People: Training and Education.

4
00:00:08.350 --> 00:00:12.689
It's for the CIO, the chief human
capital officer and HR leadership,

5
00:00:12.689 --> 00:00:17.350
the CISO, the workforce and training
leads, and the program's sponsors.

6
00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:22.176
Tools enforce the new rules, but people
make the decisions those rules depend on,

7
00:00:22.176 --> 00:00:26.637
so the modernization isn't finished
until people do their jobs the new way.

8
00:00:26.637 --> 00:00:29.588
I'll take four questions:
why people close the gap,

9
00:00:29.588 --> 00:00:34.237
what the curriculum teaches, who owns
readiness, and how to run and measure it.

10
00:00:34.237 --> 00:00:38.612
Each decision slide names the decision,
its owner and when it's needed.

11
00:00:38.612 --> 00:00:40.790
Let's start with the gap itself.

12
00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:45.490
A policy can require phishing-resistant
sign-in for every user.

13
00:00:45.490 --> 00:00:47.594
It can't decide what to do when a caller

14
00:00:47.594 --> 00:00:49.902
says they've lost their
key and need in now.

15
00:00:49.902 --> 00:00:51.815
That's the gap this book is about.

16
00:00:51.815 --> 00:00:54.538
The new estate removes
many risky settings,

17
00:00:54.538 --> 00:00:59.078
and what's left is a smaller set of
human decisions that carry more weight:

18
00:00:59.078 --> 00:01:01.271
is this caller who they say they are,

19
00:01:01.271 --> 00:01:04.827
is this access justified, is
this exception still needed.

20
00:01:04.827 --> 00:01:08.365
Someone who doesn't know the new
rule falls back on the old habit.

21
00:01:08.365 --> 00:01:12.062
So the decision for the program
office: put a training check,

22
00:01:12.062 --> 00:01:16.665
measured against a roster, into every
gate, beside the technical criteria.

23
00:01:16.665 --> 00:01:18.920
Next, whose work changes.

24
00:01:19.670 --> 00:01:23.108
Every book in this workbook
moves a decision to someone.

25
00:01:23.108 --> 00:01:26.257
The help desk now verifies
before it resets.

26
00:01:26.257 --> 00:01:29.758
Administrators activate their
roles only when they need them.

27
00:01:29.758 --> 00:01:31.182
Owners approve access,

28
00:01:31.182 --> 00:01:35.082
and HR keeps the data that creates
accounts right at the source.

29
00:01:35.082 --> 00:01:40.320
Jobs split into deciding and doing, so
two people now need each procedure.

30
00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:42.805
And decisions move to people outside IT,

31
00:01:42.805 --> 00:01:46.570
who need short training on the
one decision that's now theirs.

32
00:01:46.570 --> 00:01:51.935
So the first workforce decision belongs to
the chief human capital officer's office:

33
00:01:51.935 --> 00:01:55.001
draft the roster of everyone
whose work changes,

34
00:01:55.001 --> 00:01:58.220
from HR and identity records,
before the first gate.

35
00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:00.620
Next, why timing matters.

36
00:02:01.370 --> 00:02:03.932
A program that trains last pays twice.

37
00:02:03.932 --> 00:02:05.940
The first payment comes at cutover:

38
00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:09.194
users who weren't taught the
new sign-in call the help desk,

39
00:02:09.194 --> 00:02:12.240
and a desk that wasn't taught
the new procedure either

40
00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:15.494
turns people away or resets
accounts without the checks.

41
00:02:15.494 --> 00:02:18.408
Workarounds granted under
pressure tend to stay.

42
00:02:18.408 --> 00:02:21.671
The second payment comes later,
finding those workarounds

43
00:02:21.671 --> 00:02:25.069
and unlearning the habits, which
is slower than learning them.

44
00:02:25.069 --> 00:02:29.020
And the rules already say train
before the change, not after.

45
00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:31.395
So the decision for the sponsor and CIO:

46
00:02:31.395 --> 00:02:36.145
fund the learning program as part of the
modernization, before the first gate.

47
00:02:36.145 --> 00:02:39.680
Next, what federal workforce
rules actually require.

48
00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:44.305
Federal workforce rules are less
about courses than people expect.

49
00:02:44.305 --> 00:02:48.880
FISMA asks for trained personnel sufficient
to comply, a staffing statement,

50
00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:52.455
and enough for the old estate may
not be enough for the new one.

51
00:02:52.455 --> 00:02:58.755
A 2015 law requires every agency to
identify and code its cyber positions.

52
00:02:58.755 --> 00:03:03.248
OPM's rule sets awareness training
before access and at least once a year,

53
00:03:03.248 --> 00:03:06.693
plus training whenever
systems change significantly.

54
00:03:06.693 --> 00:03:10.205
And none of the civilian rules
requires a particular certification.

55
00:03:10.205 --> 00:03:12.820
So the decision follows Circular A-130:

56
00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:18.458
the chief human capital officer and the
CIO set competency requirements together,

57
00:03:18.458 --> 00:03:22.217
and feed the program's new
roles into the workforce plan.

58
00:03:22.217 --> 00:03:24.530
Next, the position codes.

59
00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:27.474
Position codes matter because they're the

60
00:03:27.474 --> 00:03:29.793
cleanest way to know
who needs role training.

61
00:03:29.793 --> 00:03:31.492
But they've been unreliable.

62
00:03:31.492 --> 00:03:37.371
In 2019, GAO found that 22 of 24 agencies
had given about one in five of their IT

63
00:03:37.371 --> 00:03:40.076
management positions a non-IT code,

64
00:03:40.076 --> 00:03:44.181
and over half of sampled
codes didn't match the duties.

65
00:03:44.181 --> 00:03:47.647
The program uses the code
as one input to the roster,

66
00:03:47.647 --> 00:03:51.115
beside role assignments
and the system inventory,

67
00:03:51.115 --> 00:03:55.655
and treats any disagreement as a
finding for HR and the CIO's office.

68
00:03:55.655 --> 00:03:57.782
So the decision, as program design:

69
00:03:57.782 --> 00:04:02.113
HR classification and the CIO's
office review the affected position

70
00:04:02.113 --> 00:04:06.292
descriptions and their codes as
each workstream reaches its gate.

71
00:04:06.292 --> 00:04:09.330
Next, what the curriculum
actually teaches.

72
00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:14.872
Before anyone learns a tool, the program
teaches a way of thinking: six ideas,

73
00:04:14.872 --> 00:04:18.814
starting with governance as
something that runs all the time,

74
00:04:18.814 --> 00:04:21.055
and identity as the starting point.

75
00:04:21.055 --> 00:04:24.943
Someone who holds them sees why
the help desk now asks for proof.

76
00:04:24.943 --> 00:04:28.179
Someone who doesn't feels
every new rule as friction.

77
00:04:28.179 --> 00:04:30.928
Then training teaches
the job, in four tracks:

78
00:04:30.928 --> 00:04:35.830
compliance for the people who read
evidence, implementation for the people who

79
00:04:35.830 --> 00:04:38.950
build, vendor training and
labs for product skills,

80
00:04:38.950 --> 00:04:40.955
and short modules for every user.

81
00:04:40.955 --> 00:04:44.453
The decision for the chief
human capital officer's office:

82
00:04:44.453 --> 00:04:49.043
approve a training plan that names an
owner for every track, by the first gate.

83
00:04:49.043 --> 00:04:51.180
Next, the engineers.

84
00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:56.363
Engineers get a track built on one
rule: a build isn't done when it works,

85
00:04:56.363 --> 00:05:00.330
it's done when its evidence lands
where an assessor can read it.

86
00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:01.768
Three habits come with it.

87
00:05:01.768 --> 00:05:03.466
Watch before you enforce,

88
00:05:03.466 --> 00:05:07.429
so a policy runs in report-only
before it can lock anyone out.

89
00:05:07.429 --> 00:05:11.218
Keep a way back in, with
emergency accounts created first.

90
00:05:11.218 --> 00:05:13.905
And export the record, never a screenshot.

91
00:05:13.905 --> 00:05:17.942
Each build is paired with the compliance
module that reads its evidence.

92
00:05:17.942 --> 00:05:21.347
The decision for the steward,
who owns the foundation gate:

93
00:05:21.347 --> 00:05:25.817
no gate passes until the engineers for
the next phase have passed their labs.

94
00:05:25.817 --> 00:05:29.190
Next, how the program
knows training worked.

95
00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:31.440
How do you know training worked?

96
00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:32.765
Not from attendance.

97
00:05:32.765 --> 00:05:35.203
The program certifies
what people can show.

98
00:05:35.203 --> 00:05:39.871
Each track ends in a capstone, scored
from missing up to assessor-ready,

99
00:05:39.871 --> 00:05:42.689
and the top level means
a real, dated result.

100
00:05:42.689 --> 00:05:45.094
Two program credentials come out of it,

101
00:05:45.094 --> 00:05:49.602
one for practitioners and one for
assessors, and both expire after a year.

102
00:05:49.602 --> 00:05:53.914
Industry certifications still
count as evidence of general skill,

103
00:05:53.914 --> 00:05:57.302
but they can't stand in for
the agency's own procedures.

104
00:05:57.302 --> 00:06:00.572
The decision for the CISO is
a design option from the book:

105
00:06:00.572 --> 00:06:02.683
once the training record is reliable,

106
00:06:02.683 --> 00:06:07.178
decide whether a current credential
becomes a condition for privileged roles.

107
00:06:07.178 --> 00:06:09.550
Next, where people practice.

108
00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:15.100
People need somewhere safe to practice, and
that's harder to arrange than it sounds.

109
00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:18.715
Many staff won't qualify for a
developer sandbox on their own,

110
00:06:18.715 --> 00:06:22.475
and those sandboxes don't exist
in the government clouds at all.

111
00:06:22.475 --> 00:06:26.780
So the program's approach has two steps:
practice in a commercial sandbox,

112
00:06:26.780 --> 00:06:31.515
then rehearse once in the agency's own
non-production government tenant before

113
00:06:31.515 --> 00:06:36.250
each gate, where a feature difference shows
up as a finding instead of a surprise.

114
00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:41.037
Every lab follows the same rules:
never production, never real data.

115
00:06:41.037 --> 00:06:45.069
The decision for the program office:
arrange lab tenants centrally,

116
00:06:45.069 --> 00:06:49.100
including that government rehearsal
tenant, before the first labs.

117
00:06:49.100 --> 00:06:51.660
Next, the largest group of all.

118
00:06:52.410 --> 00:06:55.810
Every user will sign in differently
by the end of the program.

119
00:06:55.810 --> 00:07:00.572
They'll learn how either from the agency,
before the change, or from an attacker,

120
00:07:00.572 --> 00:07:05.185
after it, because someone who's never seen
the new prompt can't spot a fake one.

121
00:07:05.185 --> 00:07:09.425
So end users get six short modules,
each ending in something they do:

122
00:07:09.425 --> 00:07:14.282
sign in with a phishing-resistant method,
recover an account, enroll a device,

123
00:07:14.282 --> 00:07:17.289
find their files, report
something suspicious,

124
00:07:17.289 --> 00:07:19.910
and know what the help
desk will never ask.

125
00:07:19.910 --> 00:07:24.185
The decision belongs to the CISO:
what users are taught about sign-in,

126
00:07:24.185 --> 00:07:29.285
reporting and the help desk, because those
words are the policy users actually see.

127
00:07:29.285 --> 00:07:31.320
Next, the help desk.

128
00:07:32.070 --> 00:07:35.545
The help desk is where the
new estate is most exposed.

129
00:07:35.545 --> 00:07:38.668
Attackers have phoned
employees pretending to be IT,

130
00:07:38.668 --> 00:07:41.720
and phoned the help desk
pretending to be employees.

131
00:07:41.720 --> 00:07:45.279
So the desk is trained first,
before anyone else migrates,

132
00:07:45.279 --> 00:07:49.066
and no technician gets the power
to reset a credential until

133
00:07:49.066 --> 00:07:53.382
their training and an observed
verification scenario are on record.

134
00:07:53.382 --> 00:07:58.092
Users learn the desk's rules too: it will
never ask for your password or a code,

135
00:07:58.092 --> 00:08:02.346
and it'll always let you hang up and
call back on the published number.

136
00:08:02.346 --> 00:08:05.149
The decision sits with
the service desk manager:

137
00:08:05.149 --> 00:08:08.233
whether the desk is trained
and staffed for each wave.

138
00:08:08.233 --> 00:08:10.600
Next, how waves are paced.

139
00:08:11.350 --> 00:08:12.513
Waves need a rhythm.

140
00:08:12.513 --> 00:08:15.455
Users hear about the change
about two months ahead,

141
00:08:15.455 --> 00:08:19.450
get their training a month out, and
get reminders in the last two weeks.

142
00:08:19.450 --> 00:08:24.470
Then, before anything is enforced, there's
a readiness check on three numbers:

143
00:08:24.470 --> 00:08:27.498
how many users registered
a new sign-in method,

144
00:08:27.498 --> 00:08:31.163
how many finished training,
and how busy the help desk is.

145
00:08:31.163 --> 00:08:34.225
When tickets climb, the
guidance is to slow down.

146
00:08:34.225 --> 00:08:36.833
The wave owner, identity engineering,

147
00:08:36.833 --> 00:08:40.113
makes the go or no-go, with
training now part of it.

148
00:08:40.113 --> 00:08:45.362
And the rule is simple: a wave that isn't
ready moves its date, not its threshold.

149
00:08:45.362 --> 00:08:47.650
Next, who owns all of this.

150
00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:52.744
Ask who owns training and
usually three offices answer:

151
00:08:52.744 --> 00:08:55.200
security, HR and the CIO's office.

152
00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:57.507
And nobody owns the thing that matters:

153
00:08:57.507 --> 00:09:00.463
whether people can do the
job on the day it changes.

154
00:09:00.463 --> 00:09:03.001
The book gives each decision one owner.

155
00:09:03.001 --> 00:09:06.800
The CISO owns the training policy
and what users are taught.

156
00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:11.071
The chief human capital officer's
office owns the workforce strategy,

157
00:09:11.071 --> 00:09:13.575
the position codes and the roster's facts.

158
00:09:13.575 --> 00:09:17.577
The decision for the sponsor
and CIO: name one training lead,

159
00:09:17.577 --> 00:09:22.314
and bring in the human capital office,
labor relations and the Section

160
00:09:22.314 --> 00:09:26.887
508 program manager at the first
gate, not after the first complaint.

161
00:09:26.887 --> 00:09:29.400
Next, who decides readiness.

162
00:09:30.150 --> 00:09:34.262
Readiness numbers are easy to
improve without improving readiness.

163
00:09:34.262 --> 00:09:36.387
So the book keeps the roles apart.

164
00:09:36.387 --> 00:09:38.733
The training lead reports the numbers,

165
00:09:38.733 --> 00:09:43.424
the system's security officer reviews
them, and the gate or wave owner decides.

166
00:09:43.424 --> 00:09:45.679
Managers can't waive required training,

167
00:09:45.679 --> 00:09:49.025
and phishing test results are
for learning, not discipline.

168
00:09:49.025 --> 00:09:52.788
Contractors can build, deliver
and report all of the training,

169
00:09:52.788 --> 00:09:55.887
but the decisions stay
with government officials.

170
00:09:55.887 --> 00:09:59.257
And one decision sits with the
authorizing official alone:

171
00:09:59.257 --> 00:10:03.588
whether to accept the risk of running
a system before its people are trained.

172
00:10:03.588 --> 00:10:05.912
That's a risk acceptance like any other.

173
00:10:05.912 --> 00:10:09.260
Next, how the curriculum
runs on the roadmap.

174
00:10:10.010 --> 00:10:14.172
Training has to run on the roadmap's
calendar, not the training office's.

175
00:10:14.172 --> 00:10:17.473
Each phase has people who must
be ready before its gate:

176
00:10:17.473 --> 00:10:22.092
leaders oriented before planning,
platform teams and the help desk trained

177
00:10:22.092 --> 00:10:26.198
before the foundation is built, and
every module tested on the pilot.

178
00:10:26.198 --> 00:10:30.490
And there's one step most programs
miss: when Active Directory retires,

179
00:10:30.490 --> 00:10:33.747
every course and runbook
that teaches it retires too,

180
00:10:33.747 --> 00:10:36.411
or people keep learning
a system that's gone.

181
00:10:36.411 --> 00:10:38.576
The decision for the program office:

182
00:10:38.576 --> 00:10:42.764
no gate gets scheduled without its
training dates, and no wave without

183
00:10:42.764 --> 00:10:46.735
its training window, so when a wave
slips, its training slips with it.

184
00:10:46.735 --> 00:10:49.040
Next, keeping the knowledge.

185
00:10:49.790 --> 00:10:53.540
Training is only half the job if
the knowledge walks out the door.

186
00:10:53.540 --> 00:10:55.915
The book builds in a few safeguards.

187
00:10:55.915 --> 00:10:59.629
Each module has an owner from
the team that runs the platform,

188
00:10:59.629 --> 00:11:03.565
and at least two qualified instructors,
so no one person holds it.

189
00:11:03.565 --> 00:11:07.741
Administrators of retiring systems
are trained into new roles,

190
00:11:07.741 --> 00:11:10.578
or reassigned, before
the old platform goes.

191
00:11:10.578 --> 00:11:14.834
And contractors are on the same footing
as staff: their training goes into

192
00:11:14.834 --> 00:11:18.952
the agency's own learning system, even
when their employer trained them.

193
00:11:18.952 --> 00:11:23.345
So the decision for contracting officer's
representatives: make sure every

194
00:11:23.345 --> 00:11:27.390
contractor's training is on the
agency's record before they get access.

195
00:11:27.390 --> 00:11:30.170
Next, how readiness is measured.

196
00:11:30.920 --> 00:11:33.958
Finishing a course isn't
the same as being ready.

197
00:11:33.958 --> 00:11:36.294
So the program measures three things.

198
00:11:36.294 --> 00:11:38.057
Completion, for everyone.

199
00:11:38.057 --> 00:11:41.910
Competence, for every role where
a mistake is a security event,

200
00:11:41.910 --> 00:11:45.083
like the help desk and
privileged administrators.

201
00:11:45.083 --> 00:11:48.427
And behavior, from signals
outside the learning system:

202
00:11:48.427 --> 00:11:52.862
whether users actually registered a
new sign-in method, whether help desk

203
00:11:52.862 --> 00:11:57.296
tickets settle back down after a wave,
and what people say a few weeks later.

204
00:11:57.296 --> 00:12:01.095
The pilot sets the baselines,
and each later wave adjusts them.

205
00:12:01.095 --> 00:12:06.019
The decision for the system's security
officer, against the CISO's threshold:

206
00:12:06.019 --> 00:12:11.020
anyone overdue in a privileged role is
a finding, whatever the percentage says.

207
00:12:11.020 --> 00:12:13.510
Next, what leadership should see.

208
00:12:14.260 --> 00:12:19.047
Leadership needs a short, regular
view, not a dashboard nobody acts on.

209
00:12:19.047 --> 00:12:23.009
At every steering committee meeting,
one page answers five questions:

210
00:12:23.009 --> 00:12:26.492
is the next gate's workforce ready,
are the next two waves ready,

211
00:12:26.492 --> 00:12:30.727
what happened in the last wave, where
are the gaps in privileged roles, given

212
00:12:30.727 --> 00:12:35.098
as a count rather than a percentage, and
what does the committee need to decide.

213
00:12:35.098 --> 00:12:40.255
Each quarter there's a fuller report, and
once a year the evaluation OPM requires,

214
00:12:40.255 --> 00:12:43.147
of what the training cost
and what it changed.

215
00:12:43.147 --> 00:12:47.614
The decision: the steering committee
reviews readiness at every meeting,

216
00:12:47.614 --> 00:12:50.836
and the head of the agency
owns the annual evaluation.

217
00:12:50.836 --> 00:12:53.770
Finally, the decisions in one place.

218
00:12:54.520 --> 00:12:56.808
So, the decisions in one place.

219
00:12:56.808 --> 00:13:01.344
The sponsor and CIO fund the learning
program and name a training lead.

220
00:13:01.344 --> 00:13:05.808
The program office puts training into
every gate and onto the roadmap.

221
00:13:05.808 --> 00:13:09.166
The chief human capital
officer's office owns the roster,

222
00:13:09.166 --> 00:13:11.595
the training plan and the position codes.

223
00:13:11.595 --> 00:13:13.945
The CISO decides what people are taught.

224
00:13:13.945 --> 00:13:18.265
The help desk manager and the wave owner
decide when people are actually ready,

225
00:13:18.265 --> 00:13:21.920
and the authorizing official alone
accepts the risk if they aren't.

226
00:13:21.920 --> 00:13:23.620
None of this is a procurement.

227
00:13:23.620 --> 00:13:28.308
The first step is simple: draft the
roster of everyone whose work changes.

228
00:13:28.308 --> 00:13:30.570
Tools enforce; people decide.

229
00:13:30.570 --> 00:13:34.708
Book 16 has the detail,
with every claim footnoted.

